      "level": 2,
      "content": "12.4 Panchayati Raj & Local Government"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Rajasthan was the first state in India to implement the three-tier Panchayati Raj System on 2 October 1959 (at Nagaur). Jawaharlal Nehru inaugurated it.",
      "id": "f9tim2e"
    },
    {
      "id": "giu9jln",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Tier</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Body</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Village Level</p>",
            "<p>Gram Panchayat (elected head: Sarpanch)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Block/Taluka Level</p>",
            "<p>Panchayat Samiti (elected head: Pradhan)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>District Level</p>",
            "<p>Zila Parishad (elected head: Pramukh)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Urban Local Bodies in Rajasthan include: Municipal Corporation (Nagar Nigam) – 7 cities, Municipal Council (Nagar Palika), Municipal Board (Nagar Panchayat).",
      "id": "nted0t2"
    },
    {
      "id": "po2hf5w",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "12.5 Administrative Districts (50 Districts as of 2023)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Rajasthan now has 50 districts (after reorganisation from 33). The largest district by area is Jaisalmer. The smallest district by area is Dungarpur. The most populous district is Jaipur. The least populous district is Jaisalmer.",
      "id": "bys1jg6"
    }
  ],
